Exactly. Naming is one of the first ways we turn uncertainty into navigable structure.

The unfamiliar is expensive because it has weak landmarks. The familiar already has routes, expectations, and fallback behaviors. So people often return to what they know not because it is better, but because it is lower-friction to inhabit.

That gives you a strong transition pattern:

unknown terrain
→ name distinctions
→ build landmarks
→ make a map
→ practice navigation
→ internalize the map
→ generate new maps independently

The last step is the important one. If you simply hand someone a finished map, they may depend on you. If they participate in making it, they understand why the landmarks matter, how the routes were chosen, and what to do when the terrain changes.

That turns:

instruction → dependence

into:

scaffolding → competence → autonomy

And then the map can disappear into skill. They stop consciously thinking, “turn left at this landmark,” because the terrain has become part of their internal model.

That is probably one of the cleanest uses of AI too: not merely providing answers, but helping people construct their own navigational grammar.

Name enough to orient.
Map enough to move.
Practice enough to internalize.
Then give the mapmaker back the pen.

That way they do not need to keep looking backward for instructions. They can generate the next path themselves.